body:
Found by dc on 2026-10-22, after the 3.2.5 cut, while landing 0614's lead. vc measured it from the same night's logs.

The wb verbs print every file they write on `wrote:` lines (issue 0612), and the commit rule for boards rests on that: a node commits exactly what its verbs printed, by literal path with `--only` (`intent/restart.md`). The issue verbs write tracked files too and print none of them:

- `intent issues edit <n> --from <file>` prints only `ok: issue <n> body written`.
- `intent issues close <n>` prints only `ok: issue <n> -> CLOSED`.

Each one writes an event file under `intent/.canon/events/`, the issue's canon record and its view. So a node has to find those paths in `git status` and read each one before it commits. That is the census the `wrote:` lines exist to replace. On a shared tree with a peer's writes in flight, it can also take in a path that is not its own.

MEASURED: vc's edits and closes of 0607, 0619, 0613 and 0615 on 2026-10-22 wrote eight event files, four canon records and four views, and printed none of them. dc's edit of 0614 wrote one event, the canon record and the view, and printed none of them.

NOT MEASURED: the `st`, `wp`, `ac`, `at` and `set` verbs, which also write canon. Whether they print `wrote:` is unread.

THE ASK: every verb that writes a tracked file names it on a `wrote:` line, in the shape the wb verbs print. Then the commit rule is mechanical for every verb, not only for boards.

## Spec (vc, 2026-10-23, under hv's go)

LANE: dc, S. SHAPE: first the drive, one per verb family (`issues add|edit|close`, `st new|edit|done`, `wp new|start|done`, `ac new|satisfy|edit`, `at new|green`, `set`) in a scratch project under an isolated HOME, recorded in this issue: which print `wrote:` lines and which do not. The source read at 52c373289 says only the wb arm calls the one printer at `render.rs:4866`, and the drive is what confirms it. Then the fix: every verb that writes a tracked file prints the paths it wrote through that one printer, in the wb verbs' shape, one line per path, event files included; no second format and no second printer. PROOF: a CLI arm per verb family asserting that the printed `wrote:` set equals the set of paths `git status --porcelain` shows changed in the scratch project after the verb, red on the base and green on the fix. CLOSES WHEN: the bank is judged and landed, `intent/restart.md` loses its line "a verb outside wb may print no wrote: lines" in the same bank, and `intent issues close 0624` names the sha.

## The drive (dc, 2026-10-23, vc's spec)

`refs/bank/dc/0624/drive.sh` (blob 39c0f49cffc5), log `drive.log` (65954af53031), run on the installed 3.2.5 pair (2e0e9215d). It is a scratch project under an isolated short HOME. One verb of each family runs at a time, and after each verb its `wrote:` lines are compared with what `git status --porcelain --untracked-files=all` shows changed, with a commit between verbs so each footprint stands alone.

Eighteen verbs ran: `issues add`, `edit` and `close`; `st new`, `edit`, `start` and `done`; `set` (a thread's objective); `wp new`, `start` and `done`; `ac new` (non-test and test), `edit` and `satisfy`; and `at new`, `red` and `green`. Every one exited 0, every one wrote between 3 and 6 tracked files, and NOT ONE printed a `wrote:` line. The source read at 52c373289 was right: only the `wb` arm calls the printer at `render.rs:4866`.

What each verb wrote, which is the set its `wrote:` lines will have to name:

- every verb: its event file under `intent/.canon/events/`;
- `issues add`, `edit` and `close`: the issue's canon record and its view, plus `intent/.intentfiles` for `add` and `close`;
- `st new`: the thread's canon record, `intent/st/steel_threads.md` and `intent/todo.md`;
- `st edit`, which realises the thread: `intent/.intentfiles`, `info.md` and `acceptance.md`;
- `st start` and `st done`: the canon record, `info.md`, `steel_threads.md` and `todo.md`, plus `.intentfiles` for `done`;
- `set` (objective): the canon record and `info.md`;
- `wp new`, `start` and `done`: the canon record, `WP/01/info.md`, the thread's `info.md` and `todo.md`, plus `acceptance.md` for `done`;
- `ac new`, `edit` and `satisfy`, and `at new`, `red` and `green`: the canon record and `acceptance.md`.
